---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
import IndustryPageTemplate from '../../components/IndustryPageTemplate.astro';

const pageTitle = 'Sight Glass for Chemical Processing';
const metaTitle = 'Sight Glass for Chemical Processing | Borosilicate Sight Glass — SIGHTSPEC';
const kw = 'sight glass for chemical processing';
const desc = 'Borosilicate sight glasses for chemical processing: corrosion-resistant SG-300 threaded and SG-400 flanged sight glass for reactors, pipelines and vessels handling aggressive media at high pressure.';
const points = [
  'Borosilicate glass to DIN 7080 resists most acids, alkalis and solvents without etching',
  '316 SS, Hastelloy and carbon steel bodies matched to your media, concentration and temperature',
  'SG-300 threaded rated to 100 bar (1450 psi) for compact high-pressure small-bore observation',
  'SG-400 flanged per ANSI Class 150/300 or DIN PN10–PN40 for large viewing windows on vessels and main lines',
];
const longtail = [
  'borosilicate sight glass',
  'chemical sight glass 316',
  'high pressure sight glass threaded',
  'sight glass for acid handling',
  'flanged sight glass chemical',
  'hastelloy sight glass',
  'sight glass anti fog chemical',
  'sight glass for reactor vessel',
  'sight glass for chemical pipeline',
  'DIN 7080 sight glass',
];
const recommended = [
  { href: '/products/threaded-sight-glasses/', title: 'SG-300 Threaded Sight Glass', desc: 'Male NPT/BSP 1/4"–3", borosilicate glass, up to 100 bar (1450 psi).' },
  { href: '/products/flanged-sight-glasses/', title: 'SG-400 Flanged Sight Glass', desc: 'ANSI Class 150/300 or DIN flanged, 2"–8", large viewing area.' },
  { href: '/products/sight-flow-indicators/', title: 'SG-200 Sight Flow Indicator', desc: 'Flapper, rotary or ball types for flow verification on chemical lines.' },
];
---

<Layout title={metaTitle} description={desc}>
  <IndustryPageTemplate title={pageTitle} kw={kw} desc={desc} points={points} longtail={longtail} recommended={recommended}>
    <div slot="chapters">
      <section class="bg-white">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-blue">CHEMICAL CHALLENGES</p>
          <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">Corrosion: Matching Metal and Glass to Your Media</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Chemical service attacks both halves of a sight glass: the metal housing and the glass window. The failure is rarely sudden — it is a slow etch on the glass, pitting under a gasket, or a housing specified for the wrong media. The fix starts with matching materials to the actual media, concentration and temperature, not to a generic "chemical" label.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong class="text-navy-950">Glass.</strong> Borosilicate glass to DIN 7080 is the workhorse of chemical sight glass: it resists most acids, alkalis and organic solvents, and its low thermal expansion handles the temperature swings of heated reactors. Soda-lime glass is limited to mild, low-temperature duty. Quartz is specified where process temperatures exceed borosilicate limits. A few media — hydrofluoric acid above all — attack glass itself, which is exactly why the media sheet matters before we quote.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong class="text-navy-950">Metal and seals.</strong> 316 stainless steel covers most chemical duty. Hastelloy steps in for chlorides and other aggressive media, and carbon steel is used where the media is compatible and cost matters. Seals are selected the same way: PTFE for broad chemical resistance, FKM for hydrocarbons and moderate acids, and graphite-based gaskets for high-temperature flanged service.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="bg-slate-50">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-blue">RECOMMENDED PRODUCTS</p>
          <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">SG-300 Threaded and SG-400 Flanged for Chemical Duty</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">For chemical processing we recommend two complementary models. The SG-300 threaded sight glass is the compact, high-pressure choice for small-bore lines, dosing skids and instrument connections. The SG-400 flanged sight glass provides the large viewing window needed on reactors, storage vessels and main process pipelines.</p>
          <div class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-sm">
            <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
              <thead class="bg-navy-950 text-white">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-5 py-4 font-semibold">Parameter</th>
                  <th class="px-5 py-4 font-semibold">SG-300 Threaded</th>
                  <th class="px-5 py-4 font-semibold">SG-400 Flanged</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">
                <tr><td class="px-5 py-4 font-bold text-navy-950">Mounting</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">Male NPT / BSP, 1/4"–3"</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">ANSI B16.5 Class 150/300, DIN PN10–PN40</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-5 py-4 font-bold text-navy-950">Glass</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">Borosilicate to DIN 7080</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">Borosilicate / soda lime / quartz</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-5 py-4 font-bold text-navy-950">Metal</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">316 SS / Hastelloy / carbon steel</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">Carbon steel / 316 SS</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-5 py-4 font-bold text-navy-950">Pressure</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">Up to 100 bar (1450 psi)</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">Per flange class (Class 150/300)</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="px-5 py-4 font-bold text-navy-950">Size range</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">1/4"–3"</td><td class="px-5 py-4 text-slate-600">2"–8" (DN50–DN200)</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-500">Threaded wins where space is tight, pressure is high and the bore is small. Flanged wins where operators need a real window — level checks, color checks and batch observation on vessels and main lines. Both are available with <a href="/services/oem-odm/" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue hover:underline">OEM customization</a> of glass, metal and seal.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="bg-white">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-blue">FOGGING & ETCHING</p>
          <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">Glass Fogging and Etching in Chemical Service</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Two separate problems both show up as a "dirty" window. Fogging is condensation: warm solvent or steam vapor inside the vessel meets a cooler glass surface. Etching is chemical attack: the glass surface itself is slowly eaten or roughened by the media over months of service. Fogging hides the view temporarily; etching degrades it permanently.</p>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Anti-fog options — including a heated window variant on the SG-300 and SG-400 — keep the glass above the dew point in steam and vapor service. Etching is handled at the specification stage: borosilicate for the broadest resistance, quartz where temperatures are extreme, and an honest media review when the media attacks glass. Remember that the glass window is a consumable: inspect it for etch, chips and cracks, and replace it before it becomes a leak path.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="bg-slate-50">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-blue">SELECTION GUIDE</p>
          <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">Selection Essentials for Chemical Processing</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Walk the specification in this order:</p>
          <ul class="mt-4 max-w-3xl space-y-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
            <li><strong class="text-navy-950">Media review:</strong> send the media, concentration and temperature — this decides glass, metal and seal in one pass.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-navy-950">Connection:</strong> NPT/BSP threaded for small bore and high pressure; ANSI or DIN flanged for vessels and main lines.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-navy-950">Pressure and temperature:</strong> SG-300 holds to 100 bar; SG-400 follows the flange class. State both operating and upset conditions.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-navy-950">Glass type:</strong> borosilicate DIN 7080 as standard, quartz for high temperature, soda lime only for mild duty.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-navy-950">Seal material:</strong> PTFE, FKM or graphite per media compatibility and temperature.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-navy-950">Spares:</strong> sight glass windows are consumables — keep a spare glass and gasket per size in stores.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="bg-white">
        <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-brand-blue">FAQ</p>
          <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">Chemical Sight Glass Questions</h2>
          <div class="mt-8 max-w-3xl space-y-4">
            <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
              <summary class="cursor-pointer text-base font-bold text-navy-950">Which glass resists chemicals best?</summary>
              <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Borosilicate glass to DIN 7080 resists most acids, alkalis and solvents and handles thermal shock well. Quartz is specified for very high temperatures. For media that attack glass itself — hydrofluoric acid, for example — no glass is suitable, which is why we review the media sheet before quoting.</p>
            </details>
            <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
              <summary class="cursor-pointer text-base font-bold text-navy-950">How much pressure can a chemical sight glass take?</summary>
              <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">The SG-300 threaded sight glass is rated up to 100 bar (1450 psi). The SG-400 flanged sight glass follows its flange class — ANSI Class 150 or Class 300, or DIN PN10 to PN40.</p>
            </details>
            <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
              <summary class="cursor-pointer text-base font-bold text-navy-950">Why does my sight glass fog or etch?</summary>
              <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Fogging is condensation from vapor meeting a cooler glass surface — a heated anti-fog window solves it. Etching is chemical attack that roughens the glass permanently; it is solved at specification time by choosing the right glass for your media.</p>
            </details>
            <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
              <summary class="cursor-pointer text-base font-bold text-navy-950">Threaded or flanged — which do I need?</summary>
              <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Threaded (SG-300) suits small bores, tight spaces and pressures up to 100 bar. Flanged (SG-400) suits vessels and main pipelines where you need a large viewing window and bolted access. Send your line size and we will confirm the fit.</p>
            </details>
            <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
              <summary class="cursor-pointer text-base font-bold text-navy-950">Is the glass window replaceable?</summary>
              <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Yes. The window is a replaceable, consumable part. Keep a spare glass and gasket per size in stores, and swap them on inspection. We supply replacement windows for every model we ship.</p>
            </details>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </IndustryPageTemplate>

  <CtaBand
    title="Specifying a Sight Glass for Chemical Duty?"
    subtitle="Send your media, concentration, pressure and temperature — our engineers confirm the right glass, metal and seal combination within 24 hours."
  />
</Layout>
